.\" File ID: b0b6d4a0-8e8d-11f1-b8dd-83850402c3ce
.TH LIBSELFTEST 3 "RPL_DATE" "libselftest\-RPL_VERSION"
.nh
.SH NAME
libselftest \- embedded TAP testing framework for C programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
#include "selftest.h"
.EE
.PP
libselftest is not installed as a shared or static library. Copy 
\fBselftest.c\fP and \fBselftest.h\fP into the project (\fBmake export\fP in 
the \fBsrc/\fP directory of the libselftest repository creates copies with the 
library version and date inserted), and compile \fBselftest.c\fP together with 
the program:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cc \-o prog prog.c selftest.c
.EE
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
libselftest is a small testing framework intended to be compiled directly into 
a C program. The tests are part of the binary, so they always correspond 
exactly to the program that is running, and they can call internal functions 
directly. A program typically runs its test suite when it is started with an 
option like \fB\-\-selftest\fP; see \fBlst\-demo\fP(1) for a complete example.
.PP
The test results are written to standard output in the TAP (Test Anything 
Protocol) format. Every test prints a line of the form
.PP
.RS
.EX
ok \fIN\fP \- \fIfile\fP:\fIline\fP: \fIdescription\fP
.EE
.RE
.PP
or the same line prefixed with \(lqnot \(rq if the test failed. Diagnostic 
lines start with \(lq# \(rq, and the plan line \(lq1..\fIN\fP\(rq is printed at 
the end by \fBprint_selftest_footer\fP().
.PP
Unlike most other test frameworks, all test macros and \fBlst_ok\fP() return 0 
if the test succeeds and 1 if it fails, the usual C convention. The return 
value can be added to a counter or used to print extra diagnostics when a test 
fails. All test descriptions are printf\-style format strings, followed by 
optional arguments.
.PP
The program can be compiled without the test suite by defining the 
\fBNO_SELFTEST\fP preprocessor macro, guarding the test code and the inclusion 
of \fBselftest.h\fP with \fB#ifndef NO_SELFTEST\fP, and leaving out 
\fBselftest.c\fP from the build. The library itself does not check this macro; 
it is a convention used by \fBlst\-demo\fP(1).
.PP
This page is an overview. The comments in \fBselftest.h\fP and \fBselftest.c\fP 
are the definitive reference for the exact prototypes, parameters and behaviour 
of each macro and function.
.SH MACROS
.SS Test macros
Each macro performs one test and prints one TAP line. The last parameter 
\fIdesc\fP is the printf\-style test description, followed by optional 
arguments.
.TP
\fBOK_EQUAL\fP(\fIa\fP, \fIb\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if \fIa\fP \fB==\fP \fIb\fP.
.TP
\fBOK_NOTEQUAL\fP(\fIa\fP, \fIb\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if \fIa\fP \fB!=\fP \fIb\fP.
.TP
\fBOK_TRUE\fP(\fIval\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if \fIval\fP is true (non\-zero).
.TP
\fBOK_FALSE\fP(\fIval\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if \fIval\fP is false (zero).
.TP
\fBOK_SUCCESS\fP(\fIfunc\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if \fIfunc\fP is 0, for functions that return 0 on success.
.TP
\fBOK_FAILURE\fP(\fIfunc\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if \fIfunc\fP is non\-zero, for functions that return non\-zero on 
failure.
.TP
\fBOK_NULL\fP(\fIp\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if the pointer \fIp\fP is NULL.
.TP
\fBOK_NOTNULL\fP(\fIp\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if the pointer \fIp\fP is not NULL.
.TP
\fBOK_STRCMP\fP(\fIa\fP, \fIb\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if the strings \fIa\fP and \fIb\fP are identical.
.TP
\fBOK_STRNCMP\fP(\fIa\fP, \fIb\fP, \fIlen\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if the first \fIlen\fP characters of the strings \fIa\fP and \fIb\fP 
are identical.
.TP
\fBOK_MEMCMP\fP(\fIa\fP, \fIb\fP, \fIlen\fP, \fIdesc\fP, ...)
Succeeds if the first \fIlen\fP bytes of the buffers \fIa\fP and \fIb\fP are 
identical. The buffers may contain null bytes.
.TP
\fBOK_ERROR\fP(\fIdesc\fP, ...)
Always fails. Used to report unexpected errors, typically inside a conditional.
.SS Line number variants
\fBOK_EQUAL_L\fP, \fBOK_ERROR_L\fP, \fBOK_FAILURE_L\fP, \fBOK_FALSE_L\fP, 
\fBOK_MEMCMP_L\fP, \fBOK_NOTEQUAL_L\fP, \fBOK_NOTNULL_L\fP, \fBOK_NULL_L\fP, 
\fBOK_STRCMP_L\fP, \fBOK_STRNCMP_L\fP, \fBOK_SUCCESS_L\fP and \fBOK_TRUE_L\fP 
work like the macros above, but take two extra parameters, \fIfile\fP and 
\fIlinenum\fP, right before \fIdesc\fP. They are meant for helper functions 
that are called from many places, so the TAP output can show the file name and 
line number of the caller instead of the line inside the helper function.
.SS Command tests
These macros execute \fIcmd\fP, a NULL\-terminated argument array (see 
\fBchp\fP below), and check its standard output, standard error and exit status 
against \fIexp_stdout\fP, \fIexp_stderr\fP and \fIexp_retval\fP. The last 
parameter \fIdesc\fP is the printf\-style test description, followed by 
optional arguments. Each call prints up to three TAP lines, marked 
\(lq(stdout)\(rq, \(lq(stderr)\(rq and \(lq(retval)\(rq. If \fIexp_stdout\fP or 
\fIexp_stderr\fP is NULL, that stream is not checked. An empty string does not 
skip the check, and the corresponding output must be empty. The \fBEXECSTR\fP 
macro expands to a placeholder. When that placeholder occurs in 
\fIexp_stdout\fP or \fIexp_stderr\fP, it is replaced with the value of 
\fBlst_execname\fP. The letters \fBEXECSTR\fP inside a string are not replaced. 
If Valgrind output is found in standard error, an extra failing test is 
printed. Because \fIcmd\fP is usually a compound literal containing commas, it 
must be surrounded by parentheses, for example \fBsc\fP((\fBchp\fP{ 
\fBlst_execname\fP, "\-\-version", NULL }), ...).
.TP
\fBsc\fP(\fIcmd\fP, \fIexp_stdout\fP, \fIexp_stderr\fP, \fIexp_retval\fP, ...)
The expected output strings must occur as substrings of the actual output.
.TP
\fBtc\fP(\fIcmd\fP, \fIexp_stdout\fP, \fIexp_stderr\fP, \fIexp_retval\fP, ...)
The actual output must be identical to the expected output strings.
.TP
\fBrc\fP(\fIcmd\fP, \fIexp_stdout\fP, \fIexp_stderr\fP, \fIexp_retval\fP, ...)
The actual output must match the expected strings as POSIX extended regular 
expressions. The match is unanchored. Without \fB^\fP and \fB$\fP, a match 
anywhere in the output succeeds.
.SS Output file tests
.TP
\fBverify_output_files\fP(\fIdesc\fP, \fIexp_stdout\fP, \fIexp_stderr\fP)
Checks that the standard output and standard error captured with 
\fBlst_init_output_files\fP() are identical to \fIexp_stdout\fP and 
\fIexp_stderr\fP. NULL skips the check for that stream. Note that \fIdesc\fP is 
the first parameter and is not a format string. An empty string requires the 
captured output to be empty.
.SS Diagnostic macros
.TP
\fBprint_gotexp_int\fP(\fIgot\fP, \fIexp\fP)
.TQ
\fBprint_gotexp_ulong\fP(\fIgot\fP, \fIexp\fP)
.TQ
\fBprint_gotexp_size_t\fP(\fIgot\fP, \fIexp\fP)
.TQ
\fBprint_gotexp_double\fP(\fIgot\fP, \fIexp\fP)
If the values differ, print the actual and expected values as diagnostic lines. 
Each argument is evaluated once.
.TP
\fBprint_gotexp_nostr\fP(\fIseq\fP, \fIgot\fP, \fIexp\fP)
Formats \fIgot\fP and \fIexp\fP with the printf sequence \fIseq\fP and passes 
the results to \fBprint_gotexp\fP().
.TP
\fBdiag_errno\fP()
Prints the value of \fIerrno\fP and its error message as a diagnostic line and 
resets \fIerrno\fP to 0.
.TP
\fBfailed_ok\fP(\fIa\fP)
Reports a failing test saying that \fIa\fP failed, including the function name, 
line number and the \fIerrno\fP message if \fIerrno\fP is set. Resets 
\fIerrno\fP to 0.
.TP
\fBDIAG_DEBL\fP
Prints a debug line with the current file name, function and line number.
.SS Helper macros
.TP
\fBchp\fP
Expands to the cast \fB(char *[])\fP, used to write command arrays as compound 
literals: \fBchp\fP{ "echo", "hello", NULL }.
.TP
\fBEXECSTR\fP
Macro that expands to a placeholder. \fBsc\fP, \fBtc\fP and \fBrc\fP replace 
that placeholder with \fBlst_execname\fP. Write it outside the quotes, as in 
\(lqprefix \(rq \fBEXECSTR\fP \(lq suffix\(rq.
.TP
\fBno_null\fP(\fIa\fP)
Evaluates to \fIa\fP, or to the string "(null)" if \fIa\fP is NULL.
.SS Configuration macros
.TP
\fBLST_TMPDIR\fP
Name of the temporary directory used by the tests. Defaults to 
\(lq.selftest.tmp\(rq if not defined when \fBselftest.h\fP is included. It is a 
compile\-time setting, for example
.RS
.PP
.EX
cc \-D\(aqLST_TMPDIR=".prog\-test.tmp"\(aq ...
.EE
.PP
and must have the same value in all files that are compiled with the test 
suite.
.RE
.TP
\fBLST_VERSION\fP
.TQ
\fBLST_DATE\fP
Version and date strings of the library. Inserted by \fBmake export\fP.
.SH FUNCTIONS
Only the functions meant to be called directly are listed here. The functions 
\fBsc_func\fP() and similar are the implementation behind the macros of the 
same name and should not be called directly.
.SS TAP output
.TP
\fBlst_ok\fP()
Prints an \(lqok\(rq line if its first argument is 0, otherwise a \(lqnot 
ok\(rq line, and returns 0 or 1. Used by all the test macros.
.TP
\fBlst_ok_va\fP()
Same as \fBlst_ok\fP(), but takes a \fIva_list\fP.
.TP
\fBlst_diag\fP()
Prints a printf\-style diagnostic message. Every line is prefixed with \(lq# 
\(rq, and a newline is added at the end.
.TP
\fBprint_gotexp\fP()
If the strings \fIgot\fP and \fIexp\fP differ, prints both as diagnostic lines. 
NULL is shown as \(lq(null)\(rq.
.TP
\fBlst_bail_out\fP()
Prints \(lqBail out!\(rq with an optional message and exits with 
\fBEXIT_FAILURE\fP.
.TP
\fBprint_selftest_footer\fP()
Prints the TAP plan, and a diagnostic line with the number of failed tests if 
any failed.
.TP
\fBselftest_failcount\fP()
Returns the number of failed tests so far.
.SS Test environment
.TP
\fBget_execname\fP()
Returns an allocated string suitable for \fBlst_execname\fP. If the argument 
contains a slash, the directory part is converted to an absolute path; 
otherwise, a copy is returned.
.TP
\fBlst_init_home\fP()
Sets \fBHOME\fP to the absolute path of \fBLST_TMPDIR\fP, so the tested code 
doesn't touch the real home directory. The directory is created and removed 
again to find the path, so it must not exist. Each step is reported as a test.
.TP
\fBlst_init_output_files\fP()
Redirects standard output and standard error to files in \fBLST_TMPDIR\fP, 
which must exist. Used to test functions that print.
.TP
\fBlst_restore_output_files\fP()
Restores standard output and standard error after 
\fBlst_init_output_files\fP().
.TP
\fBlst_cleanup_tempdir\fP()
Deletes the output files created by \fBlst_init_output_files\fP(), one test per 
deleted file.
.TP
\fBlst_is_root\fP()
Returns 1 if the process runs as root, otherwise 0. Useful for skipping tests 
that depend on file permissions.
.SS Command execution
.TP
\fBlst_streams_exec\fP()
Executes a command with the input in a \fBstruct lst_streams\fP as standard 
input and stores standard output, standard error and the exit status in the 
struct. Returns the exit status.
.TP
\fBlst_streams_init\fP()
.TQ
\fBlst_streams_free\fP()
Initialize or free a \fBstruct lst_streams\fP.
.SS Buffers and files
.TP
\fBlst_binbuf_init\fP()
.TQ
\fBlst_binbuf_free\fP()
Initialize or free a \fBstruct lst_binbuf\fP.
.TP
\fBlst_binbuf_cpy\fP()
Copies a \fBstruct lst_binbuf\fP, including any null bytes.
.TP
\fBlst_read_from_fp\fP()
Reads everything from a \fIFILE\fP pointer into an allocated buffer.
.TP
\fBlst_read_from_file\fP()
Returns the contents of a file as an allocated string.
.TP
\fBlst_file_exists\fP()
Returns true if the path exists. Broken symlinks count as existing.
.SS Strings
.TP
\fBlst_allocstr\fP()
.TQ
\fBlst_allocstr_va\fP()
Return an allocated string created from a printf\-style format.
.TP
\fBlst_str_replace\fP()
Returns an allocated copy of a string with all occurrences of a substring 
replaced.
.TP
\fBlst_count_substr\fP()
Returns the number of non\-overlapping occurrences of a substring.
.TP
\fBlst_re_check\fP()
Returns 0 if a string matches a POSIX extended regular expression, 1 if not, or 
\-1 if the expression is invalid. The match is unanchored. On failure to 
compile the expression, the error is written to standard error.
.SH TYPES
.TP
\fBstruct lst_binbuf\fP
A buffer that may contain null bytes, with the members \fIbuf\fP, \fIlen\fP 
(bytes used) and \fIalloc\fP (bytes allocated).
.TP
\fBstruct lst_streams\fP
Three \fBstruct lst_binbuf\fP members, \fIin\fP, \fIout\fP and \fIerr\fP, for 
standard input, output and error, and \fIret\fP, the exit status.
.SH VARIABLES
.TP
\fBlst_execname\fP
Path of the program under test. Must be set, usually with \fBget_execname\fP(), 
before \fBsc\fP, \fBtc\fP or \fBrc\fP are used. If it is NULL, those macros 
silently skip the stdout and stderr checks.
.TP
\fBlst_use_valgrind\fP
If true, \fBlst_streams_exec\fP(), and thereby \fBsc\fP, \fBtc\fP and \fBrc\fP, 
run the commands under \fBvalgrind\fP(1) with the options \fB\-q 
\-\-error\-exitcode=1 \-\-leak\-check=full \-\-show\-leak\-kinds=all\fP. 
Defaults to false.
.TP
\fBlst_testnum\fP
The number of tests executed so far.
.SH RETURN VALUE
All \fBOK_*\fP macros, \fBlst_ok\fP() and \fBlst_ok_va\fP() return 0 if the 
test succeeds and 1 if it fails.
.PP
\fBselftest_failcount\fP() returns the number of failed tests, and 
\fBprint_selftest_footer\fP() returns the number of executed tests. A test 
runner usually ends with
.PP
.RS
.EX
return selftest_failcount() ? EXIT_FAILURE : EXIT_SUCCESS;
.EE
.RE
.PP
Functions that return an allocated string return NULL on error, and the caller 
must free the result.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The library does not read any environment variables itself. 
\fBlst_init_home\fP() sets \fBHOME\fP for the running process and the commands 
it executes. Commands run by \fBlst_streams_exec\fP(), including 
\fBvalgrind\fP(1), are looked up in \fBPATH\fP by \fBexecvp\fP(3).
.PP
\fBLST_TMPDIR\fP is a preprocessor macro, not an environment variable.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fBLST_TMPDIR\fP/stdout.txt
.TQ
\fBLST_TMPDIR\fP/stderr.txt
Captured output from \fBlst_init_output_files\fP().
.SH EXAMPLES
A program that prints the sum of 2 and 3, and runs its tests when started with 
\fB\-\-selftest\fP:
.PP
.RS
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#ifndef NO_SELFTEST
#include "selftest.h"
#endif

static int add(const int a, const int b)
{
        return a + b;
}

#ifndef NO_SELFTEST
static int run_tests(const char *argv0)
{
        lst_execname = get_execname(argv0);
        if (!lst_execname)
                return EXIT_FAILURE;

        lst_diag("Test add()");
        OK_EQUAL(add(2, 3), 5, "add(2, 3) is 5");
        print_gotexp_int(add(2, 3), 5);
        OK_TRUE(add(\-1, 1) == 0, "add(%d, %d) is 0", \-1, 1);

        lst_diag("Test the executable");
        tc((chp{ lst_execname, NULL }), "5\en", "", EXIT_SUCCESS,
           "Prints the sum");

        print_selftest_footer();
        free(lst_execname);

        return selftest_failcount() ? EXIT_FAILURE : EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
#endif

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
#ifndef NO_SELFTEST
        if (argc > 1 && !strcmp(argv[1], "\-\-selftest"))
                return run_tests(argv[0]);
#else
        (void)argc;
        (void)argv;
#endif
        printf("%d\en", add(2, 3));

        return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build and run it with and without the test suite:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cc \-o sum sum.c selftest.c
\&./sum \-\-selftest
cc \-DNO_SELFTEST \-o sum sum.c
.EE
.RE
